description:
Summary:
We need a small, well-documented shim in the TypeScript SDK that enables ergonomic, correct interoperation between async iterators (for-await-of) and WHATWG Web Streams (ReadableStream) for streaming model responses. The goal is a single, tested primitive that: (1) exposes both consumption styles without duplicating memory, (2) preserves backpressure semantics across boundaries, (3) propagates cancellation cleanly, and (4) supports resumable semantics and deterministic retry for transient network errors when possible.

Background / motivation:
- Users currently struggle to support both the for-await-of async iterator and browser/streams consumers with consistent cancellation and retry behavior.
- We frequently see issues where cancellation in one model does not stop server-side work or does not inform other consumers.
- There are subtle backpressure mismatches between ReadableStream controller.desiredSize and async iterator buffering.

Goals / non-goals:
- Goals: ergonomic API, cancellation propagation via AbortSignal, single-copy payloads, minimal GC overhead, well-documented examples for Node/Browser, and a clear migration path from existing SDK helpers. Must provide a deterministic strategy for retry/resume when the service returns a resume token or sequence offset.
- Non-goals: implement a full checkpointing server; do not attempt to make non-idempotent API calls magically safe to replay.\n
Design proposal (high-level):
- Primitive: create a class StreamHandover<T> that wraps an underlying transport stream (grpc/http chunked) and exposes:
  - .getAsyncIterator(): AsyncIterableIterator<T>
  - .getReadableStream(options?): ReadableStream<T>
  - .close(reason?): Promise<void> (graceful close)
  - .resumeFrom(token: string): boolean (optional resume token)
- Backpressure mapping: when used as ReadableStream, we drive production using controller.desiredSize to throttle reads into the transport (pause-reading semantics). When used as AsyncIterator, we implement a small internal queue with a configurable high-water mark tied to desiredSize semantics; for-for-await consumers, pulling will reduce queue length and resume reads. If both APIs are consumed concurrently, the shim enforces a single canonical pull loop and maps both consumers onto the same queue.
- Cancellation propagation: an AbortController is created per StreamHandover instance. Consumers may pass an AbortSignal to either consumer surface. Abort causes: (a) stop reading from transport, (b) forward a cancellation message to transport if supported, (c) drain outstanding queued items depending on close mode (graceful vs immediate).
- Retry & resume semantics: require transports to optionally emit resumeToken metadata in chunk envelopes. On transient errors, StreamHandover will attempt a short backoff retry up to N attempts (configurable) and call resumeFrom(token) to request the server to skip already-processed tokens. This is only allowed if the request is considered idempotent; the consumer must opt-in via options.idempotent = true.
- Error model & guarantees: the shim will provide strong ordering guarantees for emitted chunks and will never duplicate messages exposed to a single consumer except during resume windows where duplicate filtering by sequence id is applied. If deterministic resume is unavailable, we fail fast and surface an explicit non-retriable error.

API ergonomics examples (short):
- for-await-of:
  const handover = new StreamHandover(transport, { idempotent: true });
  for await (const chunk of handover.getAsyncIterator()) {
    // chunk is the unified chunk envelope { seq, data, resumeToken? }
  }

- ReadableStream:
  const rs = handover.getReadableStream();
  const reader = rs.getReader();
  while (true) {
    const { value, done } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    // process value
  }

- Cancellation:
  const ac = new AbortController();
  const iterator = handover.getAsyncIterator({ signal: ac.signal });
  // consumer calls ac.abort() to cancel both stream flavors

Testing & performance:
- Unit tests: concurrency (two concurrent consumers), cancellation propagation, desiredSize mapping, resume token handling, deduplication of sequence ids, and retry backoff windows.
- Integration tests: run with our local streaming test server that supports explicit resume tokens and simulated transient errors. Add a stress test that measures throughput vs memory when bridging to both consumers concurrently.
- Performance targets: overhead of shim should be <1-2% median latency increase and <5% tail compared to raw transport when consumers use single access style. Memory overhead for dual consumers should remain bounded by configurable highWaterMark (default 8 items).

Alternatives considered:
- Provide two separate helpers (one for async iterator, one for ReadableStream) and force users to pick. Rejected because duplication leads to cancellation mismatches and increased memory usage if both are used.
- Always buffering all incoming chunks in a ring buffer. Rejected because it defeats backpressure guarantees in browser contexts.

Acceptance criteria:
- A StreamHandover implementation that passes unit and integration tests listed above.
- Documentation page with 2 examples (Node async iterator, browser readable stream) and migration notes.
- Benchmark showing <5% memory overhead and <2% median latency overhead for common patterns.
- PR with code, tests, and docs merged into main and a short migration guide published to docs.redwood.com.
